;;; DRAFT -- drop-right!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/drop-right-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "drop-right!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(drop-right! lst k) -> list"
  :summary "All but the last k elements of lst, produced by cutting the spine rather than copying it. The argument is destroyed."
  :explanation "drop-right! and take! arrive at the same place from opposite directions: take! is told how many to keep, drop-right! is told how many to lose. Both cut the spine once and return the argument's own head cell -- the suite checks (eq? x y) for drop-right! at list.sld:315-320 exactly as it does for take!. The difference that matters is arithmetic, not structure: drop-right! must walk the list to learn its length before it knows where to cut, so it is not cheaper than drop-right in traversal, only in allocation. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. Note the asymmetry with drop-right's pure sibling: drop-right can afford to share the argument's cells because it never writes; drop-right! writes, so sharing becomes destruction."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (drop-right! xs 3))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the argument is the result: xs has lost its last three.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define head (drop-right! xs 3))\n(display (list 'head head 'xs xs 'same (eq? head xs)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; k = 0 keeps everything; k = length gives the empty list and there is\n;; then no cell to return, so the argument binding is the only casualty.\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(display (list 'k0 (drop-right! a 0) 'kall (drop-right! b 3)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed. list.sld:315-320 asserts the result is eq? to the argument and shares its cells down to the cut."
             "It must traverse the whole list to find the cut point, so it saves allocation, not time."
             "k greater than the length is an error; there is no negative-length list to return."
             "The elements that were dropped are unreachable afterwards. Unlike split-at!, nothing hands them back.")
  :drawbacks ("Two traversals of the argument in the obvious implementation -- one to measure, one to cut -- against drop-right's one-and-a-copy."
             "Correctness depends on a fact the type system cannot state: that this list has exactly one owner."
             "It reads as an accessor at the call site while behaving as a command.")
  :usecases ("Stripping a known-length trailer (a checksum, a sentinel run) off a buffer you just assembled."
             "Shrinking a scratch accumulator in a loop where the discarded tail is dead by construction."
             "Symmetric partner to take! when the count you have is a count of what to lose.")
  :related ("drop-right"
             "take!"
             "take-right"
             "split-at!"
             "last-pair")
  :learn (:concept "Cutting from the far end, and why that costs a traversal"
          :prerequisites ("drop-right" "length" "set-cdr!" "take!")
          :progression "Understand drop-right and take! as the two ways to name one cut, then learn why only one of them can be O(1) in a singly-linked list.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:315-320; signature derived from the drop-right record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (drop-right! xs 3))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define b (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (equal? (drop-right a 3) (drop-right! b 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define head (drop-right! xs 3))\n(display (eq? head xs))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a buffer with a fixed-size trailer to strip. Candidates: drop-right (copies the part it keeps, leaves the buffer whole), take with a computed count (same cost, one more arithmetic step to get wrong), or drop-right! (no allocation, buffer destroyed). Because the count of what to LOSE is what the operator holds, drop-right! reads more directly than take! here -- and that readability is the trap, since the two verbs differ in danger, not in clarity."
     :explanation "eng: the trailer is gone and the buffer variable now names the stripped list. math: drop-right!(l,k) = drop-right(l,k) as a value, for every proper l and legal k. cs: the eq? check is the only observable difference, and no ordinary test looks for it."
     :meta "When two verbs differ only in an effect no assertion in your suite examines, the suite is not telling you which one you called.")
    (:eng "Naming the cut by what you lose is clearer here, and clarity is not safety."
     :math "The two directions of one cut agree on values for all legal arguments."
     :cs "A difference nothing observes is a difference that ships.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Removing a fixed-size tail from a sequence by severing it where it lies."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (drop-right! (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a message with a trailing checksum" :code "(define frame (list 'h 'd1 'd2 'sum))\n(display (drop-right! frame 1))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a queue that forgets its oldest" :code "(define (forget-oldest! q n) (drop-right! q n))\n(display (forget-oldest! (list 'new 'mid 'old) 1))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the cut lands k from the end. A frame with a checksum is the same operation given a protocol name: strip the trailer, keep the payload, and the trailer is not wanted again. A queue that forgets its oldest entries is the same cut applied to time. All three share a tail that is known-dead before the call."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the discarded suffix has already been consumed or was never wanted.")
    (:eng "If the tail were wanted, this would be the wrong verb; split-at! would be the right one."
     :math "Suffix removal is prefix retention with the count taken from the other end."
     :cs "Protocol framing is the everyday instance: header, payload, trailer, and only the payload survives.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define head (drop-right! xs 3))\n(display (list 'head head 'len (length head)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (agree? n k)\n  (let loop ((i n) (a (list)) (b (list)))\n    (if (= i 0)\n        (equal? (drop-right a k) (drop-right! b k))\n        (loop (- i 1) (cons i a) (cons i b)))))\n(display (list (agree? 6 3) (agree? 3 0) (agree? 3 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define alias xs)\n(define head (drop-right! xs 1))\n(display (list 'alias-shortened-too (eq? alias head)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a proper list lst and 0 <= k <= (length lst), drop-right! returns a list equal? to (drop-right lst k); for k < (length lst) that result is eq? to lst. take!(lst, |lst|-k) and drop-right!(lst, k) name the same cut."
     :invariant "forall proper lst, 0 <= k <= |lst|. equal?(drop-right!(lst,k), drop-right(lst,k)) and equal?(drop-right!(lst,k), take(lst, |lst| - k))"
     :meta "The pattern is one cut, two coordinate systems. What would falsify the claim: disagreement with drop-right at either boundary (k = 0, k = |lst|), or a fresh list where the argument's head cell was expected.")
    (:eng "State the invariant in both coordinate systems and the off-by-one has nowhere to hide."
     :math "The identity drop-right(l,k) = take(l,|l|-k) is what lets one implementation serve both names."
     :cs "The k = 0 and k = |lst| boundaries are where linear-update verbs most often forget to return the argument.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (strip-trailer! frame trailer-size)\n  (drop-right! frame trailer-size))\n(display (strip-trailer! (list 'h 'd 'sum) 1))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (middle! xs front back)\n  (drop-right! (drop xs front) back))\n(display (middle! (list 1 2 3 4 5 6) 1 2))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (rotate-drop xs k)\n  (append (drop xs k) (drop-right xs (- (length xs) k))))\n(display (rotate-drop (list 1 2 3 4) 2))\n(newline)"
     :base "drop-right! cuts k cells off the end of a list and returns what is left of the argument."
     :emergent "eng: trailer stripping, the protocol-shaped use. math: composing drop with drop-right! carves a middle segment, and note the pure drop must run FIRST -- after the destructive cut there is no original left to drop from. cs: the pure rotation shows what the same arithmetic looks like when nothing is destroyed, and it is the version you can call twice."
     :meta "The family is segment extraction. What emerges is a rule: in a pipeline mixing pure and linear-update verbs, every pure read must precede the first destructive write.")
    (:eng "One destructive step in a pipeline fixes the order of every step around it."
     :math "Front and back offsets compose into a middle segment; the algebra is unaffected by which verbs are destructive."
     :cs "Idempotence is the casualty. drop-right! called twice on the same variable does not mean what it means once.")
  )
)
